```
This code reads the contents of a CSS file using the `fs` module, parses it using the `csstria` library, and then removes any detected browser hacks based on a predefined list of known hacks before outputting the cleaned CSS content into a new file. The `removeBrowserHacks()` method is used to remove any browser-specific hacks that are present in the CSS content.

```
const fs = require('fs');
const csstria = require('csstria');

// Read the contents of the CSS file
const cssContent = fs.readFileSync('path/to/css/file.css', 'utf8');

// Parse the CSS content using Csstria
const parsedCss = csstria.parse(cssContent);

// Define a list of known browser hacks
const knownHacks = [
  {
    name: 'IE hack',
    regex: /\\0\/|\\1\/|\\2\/|\\3\/|\\4\/|\\5\/|\\6\/|\\7\//g,
  },
  {
    name: 'Firefox hack',
    regex: /@-moz-document url-prefix\(\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\"\)|@-moz-document url-prefix\(\"https:\/\/www.w3.org\"\)/g,
  },
];

// Remove any detected browser hacks from the parsed CSS content
parsedCss.forEach((rule) => {
  knownHacks.forEach((hack) => {
    if (rule.selectorText.match(hack.regex)) {
      rule.remove();
    }
  });
});

// Output the cleaned CSS content into a new file
fs.writeFileSync('path/to/output/file.css', parsedCss.toString());
```

