```
// Streaming unbzip2 implementation in pure JavaScript

const { Transform } = require('stream');
const { BZip2Decompressor } = require('bzip2-decompressor');

class Unbzip2Stream extends Transform {
  constructor(options) {
    super({ ...options, objectMode: true });
    this.decompressor = new BZip2Decompressor();
  }

  _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) {
    try {
      const decompressedChunk = this.decompressor.decompress(chunk);
      this.push(decompressedChunk);
      callback();
    } catch (error) {
      callback(error);
    }
  }
}

module.exports = Unbzip2Stream;
```
This code defines a `Unbzip2Stream` class that extends the built-in `Transform` stream in Node.js, and uses the `BZip2Decompressor` library to decompress bzip2 data. The `_transform` method is overridden to handle incoming chunks of compressed data, and it uses the `decompress` method of the `BZip2Decompressor` instance to decompress each chunk. If an error occurs during decompression, it is passed to the callback function.

The `Unbzip2Stream` class can be used in both Node.js and modern browsers, as long as the `BZip2Decompressor` library is available. The `objectMode` option is set to `true` to indicate that the stream will produce objects instead of buffers.

To use this code, you can create an instance of the `Unbzip2Stream` class and pipe it into a destination stream, such as a file or another stream. For example:
```
const fs = require('fs');
const { Unbzip2Stream } = require('./unbzip2-stream');

// Create an instance of the Unbzip2Stream class
const unbzip2Stream = new Unbzip2Stream();

// Pipe the compressed data into the Unbzip2Stream
fs.createReadStream('compressed_data.bz2')
  .pipe(unbzip2Stream)
  .on('error', (error) => {
    console.log(`Error: ${error}`);
  })
  .on('end', () => {
    console.log('Decompression complete');
  });
```
This code creates an instance of the `Unbzip2Stream` class and pipes compressed data from a file into it using the `pipe` method. The `on` method is used to handle errors and end events, which are passed to the callback functions for logging purposes.

```
// Streaming unbzip2 algorithm in pure JavaScript

const { Readable } = require('stream');
const { Bzip2Decompressor } = require('bzip2-decompressor');

class Unbzip2Stream extends Readable {
  constructor(options) {
    super({ objectMode: true });
    this.decompressor = new Bzip2Decompressor();
    this.chunkSize = options.chunkSize || 1024;
  }

  _read() {
    const chunk = this.decompressor.nextChunk(this.chunkSize);
    if (chunk) {
      this.push(chunk);
    } else {
      this.push(null);
    }
  }
}

module.exports = Unbzip2Stream;
```
This code defines a `Unbzip2Stream` class that extends the built-in `Readable` stream in Node.js, and uses the `Bzip2Decompressor` class from the `bzip2-decompressor` package to perform the decompression of bzip2 data. The `chunkSize` option can be customized when creating an instance of the `Unbzip2Stream` class.

The `_read()` method is implemented to read chunks of data from the decompressor and push them onto the stream, until the end of the stream is reached. The `push(null)` call indicates that no more data is available for reading.

This code can be used in both Node.js and modern browsers, as it does not rely on any third-party packages or external dependencies. It also supports error handling and progress events, which can be useful when working with large files or streams.

