```
const hast = require('hast-util');

function parse5ToHast(parse5Ast) {
  const hastAst = {};

  // Iterate over each node in the Parse5 AST
  for (const node of parse5Ast.children) {
    // Check if the current node is a text node
    if (node.type === 'text') {
      // Add the text to the hast AST as a text node
      hastAst.push({ type: 'text', value: node.value });
    } else if (node.type === 'element') {
      // Create a new hast element object for the current node
      const element = {};

      // Add the tag name and attributes to the hast element object
      element.tagName = node.name;
      element.attributes = node.attribs;

      // Iterate over each child node of the current node
      for (const childNode of node.children) {
        // Recursively call the parse5ToHast function on the child node
        const childElement = parse5ToHast(childNode);

        // Add the child element to the hast element object
        element.children.push(childElement);
      }

      // Add the hast element object to the hast AST
      hastAst.push(element);
    }
  }

  return hastAst;
}
```
This code uses the `hast-util` library to transform a Parse5 AST into a HAST AST, which allows for easier manipulation and optimization of the HTML code. The `parse5ToHast` function takes in a Parse5 AST as an argument and returns a HAST AST.

The function iterates over each node in the Parse5 AST using a `for...of` loop, and checks if the current node is a text node or an element node. If it's a text node, it adds the text to the hast AST as a text node. If it's an element node, it creates a new hast element object for the current node, adds the tag name and attributes to the hast element object, and recursively calls the `parse5ToHast` function on each child node of the current node. It then adds the hast element object to the hast AST.

The resulting HAST AST can be easily manipulated and optimized using the `hast-util` library, allowing for more efficient processing of HTML code.

