```
import { useState } from 'react';
import { DocGen } from '@docgen/core';
import { TypeScript } from '@docgen/typescript';

const App = () => {
  const [sourceFiles, setSourceFiles] = useState([]);
  const [documentation, setDocumentation] = useState({});

  const handleDirectoryChange = (event) => {
    const files = event.target.files;
    if (files.length > 0) {
      const sourceFiles = Array.from(files).map((file) => file.path);
      setSourceFiles(sourceFiles);
    }
  };

  const handleGenerateDocumentation = () => {
    DocGen.generate({
      type: 'typescript',
      files: sourceFiles,
      output: documentation,
    });
    setDocumentation(documentation);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Doc Generation Tool</h1>
      <input type="file" onChange={handleDirectoryChange} />
      <button onClick={handleGenerateDocumentation}>Generate Documentation</button>
      {documentation && (
        <div>
          <h2>Documentation</h2>
          <p>{documentation.title}</p>
          <p>{documentation.description}</p>
          <ul>
            {documentation.sections.map((section) => (
              <li key={section.id}>{section.name}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};
```
This code creates a React component that uses the `useState` hook to manage the state of the source files and the generated documentation. The `handleDirectoryChange` function is called when the user selects a directory of source files, and it updates the `sourceFiles` state with the selected files. The `handleGenerateDocumentation` function is called when the user clicks the "Generate Documentation" button, and it uses the `DocGen` library to generate the documentation for the selected source files. The generated documentation is then displayed in the UI.

Note that this code assumes that you have already installed the necessary dependencies, including React, DocGen, and TypeScript. You will also need to configure your project to use TypeScript as the default language for your components.

```
import { createElement } from 'react';
import { DocGen } from '@docgen/core';
import { TypeScript } from '@typescript/language';

const docGen = new DocGen({
  input: './src',
  output: './docs',
  language: TypeScript,
});

const app = createElement(docGen);

ReactDOM.render(app, document.getElementById('root'));
```
This code creates a React application that uses the DocGen library to generate documentation for a directory of source files written in TypeScript. The `input` property is set to `./src`, which specifies the directory containing the source files. The `output` property is set to `./docs`, which specifies the directory where the generated documentation will be saved. The `language` property is set to `TypeScript`, which specifies that the source files are written in TypeScript.

The `createElement` function is used to create a React element that wraps the DocGen component. This allows us to render the DocGen component within a React application.

Finally, we use the `ReactDOM.render` function to render the React element within the HTML document. The `document.getElementById('root')` selector specifies the element where the React application will be rendered.

