```
const { Tracer } = require('opentracing');
const { ConsoleLogger } = require('opentracing-logger');

class NodeOpenTracingProvider {
  constructor() {
    this.tracers = new Map();
  }

  register(name, tracer) {
    this.tracers.set(name, tracer);
  }

  getTracer(name) {
    return this.tracers.get(name);
  }

  publish(span) {
    const tracer = this.getTracer(span.traceId);
    if (tracer) {
      tracer.publish(span);
    } else {
      console.error(`No tracer found for trace ID ${span.traceId}`);
    }
  }
}

const provider = new NodeOpenTracingProvider();

// Register a tracer with the provider
provider.register('my-tracer', new Tracer({
  logger: new ConsoleLogger(),
  propagationFormat: 'b3' // Supports multiple propagation formats
}));

// Get a tracer from the provider
const myTracer = provider.getTracer('my-tracer');

// Create a span and publish it to the OpenTracing collector
const span = myTracer.startSpan('my-span');
provider.publish(span);
```
This code creates a `NodeOpenTracingProvider` class that manages tracers for a Node.js application. It provides methods for registering and retrieving tracers, as well as publishing spans to the OpenTracing collector. The provider also supports multiple propagation formats through the `propagationFormat` option in the `Tracer` constructor.

To use this code, you would first need to install the `opentracing` and `opentracing-logger` packages using npm or yarn. Then, you can create an instance of the `NodeOpenTracingProvider` class and register tracers with it using the `register` method. You can then retrieve a tracer from the provider using the `getTracer` method and use it to start spans and publish them to the OpenTracing collector using the `publish` method.

Note that this code does not include any error handling or logging for failed span publication, so you may want to add those features depending on your specific use case.

