To rebase or inline URLs in CSS using a PostCSS plugin, you can use the following code:
```javascript
const postcss = require('postcss');
const urlRebase = require('postcss-url-rebase');

const css = `
  body {
    background-image: url(https://example.com/image.jpg);
  }
`;

const output = postcss()
  .use(urlRebase({
    basePath: 'https://example.com',
    inline: true,
  }))
  .process(css)
  .then((result) => {
    return result.css;
  });
```
This code uses the `postcss` module to process a CSS string and the `urlRebase` plugin to rebase or inline URLs in the CSS. The `basePath` option is set to `'https://example.com'` to indicate that all URLs should be rebased to this base path. The `inline` option is set to `true` to indicate that any URL that can be inlined should be inlined.

The resulting output will be a CSS string with the URLs rebased or inlined as appropriate. For example, if the input CSS contained a URL like `url(https://example.com/image.jpg)`, the output would contain the inlined image data instead of a reference to the external URL.

Here is an example of how you can use a PostCSS plugin to rebase or inline URLs in CSS:
```
const postcss = require('postcss');
const urlRebase = require('url-rebase');

// Define the CSS input and output files
const cssInputFile = 'input.css';
const cssOutputFile = 'output.css';

// Read the CSS input file
const cssInput = fs.readFileSync(cssInputFile, 'utf8');

// Use the url-rebase plugin to rebase or inline URLs in the CSS
const cssOutput = postcss([urlRebase()]).process(cssInput);

// Write the output CSS file
fs.writeFileSync(cssOutputFile, cssOutput);
```
This code uses the `postcss` module to process the input CSS file and the `url-rebase` plugin to rebase or inline URLs in the CSS. The resulting output CSS is written to a separate file.

You can also use other PostCSS plugins such as `postcss-url` which allows you to inline images, fonts, and other assets directly into your CSS files.
