Here is an example of how you could implement an ESLint plugin for Angular applications that checks for consistent naming conventions in component files:
```
// eslint-plugin-angular-naming-conventions.js

module.exports = {
  rules: {
    'angular-naming-conventions': {
      create(context) {
        return {
          ClassDeclaration(node) {
            if (node.superClass && node.superClass.name === 'Component') {
              const componentName = node.id.name;
              const recommendedFormat = componentName.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + componentName.slice(1);
              if (componentName !== recommendedFormat) {
                context.report({
                  node,
                  message: `Component name '${componentName}' does not match the recommended format '${recommendedFormat}'.`,
                  fix(fixer) {
                    return fixer.replaceText(node.id, recommendedFormat);
                  }
                });
              }
            }
          }
        };
      }
    }
  }
};
```
This plugin defines a new rule called `angular-naming-conventions` that checks for consistent naming conventions in component files. The rule is applied to all class declarations that extend the `Component` class. If the name of the component does not match the recommended format (i.e., starting with an uppercase letter), the plugin will report a violation and suggest a fix by renaming the component to match the recommended format.

To use this plugin, you would need to install it in your project using npm or yarn:
```
npm install eslint-plugin-angular-naming-conventions
```
Once installed, you can enable the rule in your ESLint configuration file (e.g., `.eslintrc.json`):
```
{
  "plugins": [
    "angular-naming-conventions"
  ],
  "rules": {
    "angular-naming-conventions/angular-naming-conventions": "error"
  }
}
```
This will enable the rule and report any violations found in your code. You can also customize the recommended format for component names by modifying the `recommendedFormat` variable in the plugin's code.

