Here is an example of how you could create a Salesforce CLI plugin using only built-in or standard capabilities in JavaScript:
```
const { Command } = require('commander');
const { SalesforceClient } = require('salesforce-client');

// Create a new command for the plugin
const salesforceCommand = new Command();
salesforceCommand.name('salesforce').description('Perform various Salesforce-related tasks');

// Add subcommands to the command
salesforceCommand.command('retrieve <object>')
  .description('Retrieve data from a Salesforce object')
  .action(async (object) => {
    const client = new SalesforceClient();
    const result = await client.query(`SELECT * FROM ${object}`);
    console.log(result);
  });

salesforceCommand.command('manipulate <object>')
  .description('Manipulate data in a Salesforce object')
  .action(async (object) => {
    const client = new SalesforceClient();
    const result = await client.query(`SELECT * FROM ${object}`);
    console.log(result);
  });

salesforceCommand.command('dependencies <object>')
  .description('Manage dependencies in a Salesforce object')
  .action(async (object) => {
    const client = new SalesforceClient();
    const result = await client.query(`SELECT * FROM ${object}`);
    console.log(result);
  });

salesforceCommand.command('api <endpoint>')
  .description('Interact with the Salesforce API')
  .action(async (endpoint) => {
    const client = new SalesforceClient();
    const result = await client.query(`SELECT * FROM ${object}`);
    console.log(result);
  });

// Add a help command to the plugin
salesforceCommand.command('help')
  .description('Display help for the salesforce command')
  .action(() => {
    console.log('Usage: salesforce <command> [options]');
    console.log();
    console.log('Commands:');
    console.log('  retrieve <object>   Retrieve data from a Salesforce object');
    console.log('  manipulate <object> Manipulate data in a Salesforce object');
    console.log('  dependencies <object> Manage dependencies in a Salesforce object');
    console.log('  api <endpoint>     Interact with the Salesforce API');
    console.log();
    console.log('Options:');
    console.log('  --help             Display help for the salesforce command');
  });

// Run the plugin
salesforceCommand.parse(process.argv);
```
This code creates a new `Command` object using the `commander` package, which is a built-in module in Node.js. The `Command` object represents the root of the command hierarchy and provides methods for defining subcommands and options.

The `salesforceCommand` object defines four subcommands: `retrieve`, `manipulate`, `dependencies`, and `api`. Each subcommand has a description and an action function that is called when the subcommand is invoked. The action functions use the `SalesforceClient` class from the `salesforce-client` package to interact with the Salesforce API.

The `help` command is also defined using the `Command` object, and it displays help for the `salesforce` command when invoked.

Finally, the code runs the plugin by calling the `parse()` method on the `salesforceCommand` object, which parses the command line arguments and invokes the appropriate subcommand or option.

