// modules/bars.js
import Component from '@ember/component';
import Router from '@ember/routing/router';

export default class Bars extends Component {
  // component for rendering a bar with dynamic title and configurable height
  static positionalParams = ['title', 'height'];

  getTitle() {
    return this.get('title');
  }

  getHeight() {
    return this.get('height');
  }
}

export default class BarsRouter extends Router {
  // router for updating the title and height based on the current route
  location = 'none';

  routes = {
    '/': {
      template: '<bars @title="Home" @height="100px">'
    },
    '/about': {
      template: '<bars @title="About" @height="200px">'
    }
  };
}
```
This code creates a module for adding HTML bars to an Ember CLI project, including a component that renders a bar with a dynamic title and a configurable height, and a router that updates the title and height based on the current route. The `Bars` component is defined using the `@ember/component` class, which allows us to define the component's template and its positional parameters (in this case, `title` and `height`). The `getTitle()` and `getHeight()` methods are used to retrieve the values of these parameters.

The `BarsRouter` class is defined using the `@ember/routing/router` class, which allows us to define routes for our application. In this case, we have two routes: `/` and `/about`. The template for each route is a `<bars>` component with different values for the `title` and `height` parameters.

Note that this code assumes that you have already set up an Ember CLI project and are using the `@ember/component` and `@ember/routing/router` classes in your application. If you haven't, you can learn more about how to do so by reading the Ember documentation on components and routers.

