```
import React from 'react';
import { useState } from 'react';
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import { useRef } from 'react';
import { GoogleReCAPTCHA } from '@google-cloud/recaptcha';

const Recaptcha = ({ siteKey, onVerify }) => {
  const [token, setToken] = useState(null);
  const recaptchaRef = useRef();

  useEffect(() => {
    if (recaptchaRef.current) {
      const recaptcha = new GoogleReCAPTCHA(recaptchaRef.current);
      recaptcha.execute().then((token) => setToken(token));
    }
  }, [recaptchaRef]);

  return (
    <div>
      <div ref={recaptchaRef} />
      {token && onVerify(token)}
    </div>
  );
};
```
This code defines a `Recaptcha` component that wraps the Google reCAPTCHA API. The component takes two props: `siteKey`, which is the site key provided by Google, and `onVerify`, which is a callback function that will be called with the token returned from the reCAPTCHA API when the user successfully completes the captcha challenge.

The component uses the `useState` hook to keep track of the token value, and the `useRef` hook to create a reference to the reCAPTCHA element. The `useEffect` hook is used to execute the reCAPTCHA API when the component mounts, and to update the token value when the user completes the captcha challenge.

The component returns a div element that contains the reCAPTCHA element and a callback function that will be called with the token value when the user successfully completes the captcha challenge.

