```
const AbstractLevelDOWN = require('abstract-leveldown');
const IndexedDBStore = require('indexeddb-store');

class IndexedDBLevelDOWN extends AbstractLevelDOWN {
  constructor(location) {
    super();
    this.location = location;
    this.db = new IndexedDBStore(this.location);
  }

  async open() {
    await this.db.open();
  }

  async close() {
    await this.db.close();
  }

  async put(key, value) {
    await this.db.put(key, value);
  }

  async get(key) {
    return await this.db.get(key);
  }

  async del(key) {
    await this.db.del(key);
  }

  async batch(array) {
    await this.db.batch(array);
  }
}
```
This code creates a class called `IndexedDBLevelDOWN` that extends the `AbstractLevelDOWN` class from the `abstract-leveldown` library. It also requires the `indexeddb-store` library to provide a simple key-value storage API similar to leveldown, but utilizing the indexeddb library for storage.

The constructor of the class takes a location parameter and initializes an instance of the `IndexedDBStore` class with that location. The `open`, `close`, `put`, `get`, `del`, and `batch` methods are implemented to provide a simple key-value storage API similar to leveldown, but utilizing the indexeddb library for storage.

The `open` method is called when the store needs to be opened, and it opens the underlying IndexedDB database using the `IndexedDBStore.open()` method. The `close` method is called when the store needs to be closed, and it closes the underlying IndexedDB database using the `IndexedDBStore.close()` method.

The `put`, `get`, `del`, and `batch` methods are used to perform CRUD operations on the key-value pairs in the store. The `put` method is called when a new key-value pair needs to be added to the store, and it adds the key-value pair using the `IndexedDBStore.put()` method. The `get` method is called when a key-value pair needs to be retrieved from the store, and it retrieves the value associated with the given key using the `IndexedDBStore.get()` method. The `del` method is called when a key-value pair needs to be deleted from the store, and it deletes the key-value pair using the `IndexedDBStore.del()` method. The `batch` method is called when multiple operations need to be performed on the store at once, and it performs the operations using the `IndexedDBStore.batch()` method.

Note that this code assumes that the `indexeddb-store` library has been installed as a dependency in your project.

Here's some sample code for creating an interactive graph visualization tool using a graph theory library in JavaScript:
```
// Import the necessary libraries
import { Graph } from 'graph-theory';
import * as d3 from 'd3';

// Create a new instance of the Graph class
const graph = new Graph();

// Add nodes and edges to the graph
graph.addNode('A');
graph.addNode('B');
graph.addEdge('A', 'B');

// Define the layouts and algorithms available for the graph
const layouts = {
  'Spring Layout': () => d3.forceSimulation(graph.nodes)
    .force('charge', d3.forceManyBody().strength(-10))
    .force('link', d3.forceLink(graph.edges).distance(50)),
  'Circular Layout': () => d3.forceSimulation(graph.nodes)
    .force('charge', d3.forceManyBody().strength(-10))
    .force('center', d3.forceCenter())
};
const algorithms = {
  'Breadth-First Search': (start, end) => graph.breadthFirstSearch(start, end),
  'Depth-First Search': (start, end) => graph.depthFirstSearch(start, end)
};

// Create an interactive visualization of the graph using D3.js
const svg = d3.select('svg');
const nodeRadius = 10;
const edgeWidth = 2;

// Define the styles for the nodes and edges
const nodeStyle = {
  fill: 'lightblue',
  stroke: 'black',
  'stroke-width': 2,
  rx: nodeRadius,
  ry: nodeRadius
};
const edgeStyle = {
  stroke: 'gray',
  'stroke-width': edgeWidth
};

// Add the nodes and edges to the visualization
graph.nodes.forEach(node => {
  const circle = svg.append('circle')
    .attr('cx', node.x)
    .attr('cy', node.y)
    .attr('r', nodeRadius)
    .style(nodeStyle);
});
graph.edges.forEach(edge => {
  const line = svg.append('line')
    .attr('x1', edge.source.x)
    .attr('y1', edge.source.y)
    .attr('x2', edge.target.x)
    .attr('y2', edge.target.y)
    .style(edgeStyle);
});

// Add event listeners for user input
svg.on('click', () => {
  // Get the coordinates of the click event
  const [x, y] = d3.mouse(svg.node());

  // Find the node that was clicked on
  const node = graph.nodes.find(n => n.x === x && n.y === y);

  if (node) {
    // Highlight the clicked node and its neighbors
    svg.selectAll('circle')
      .filter(d => d.id === node.id || graph.neighbors(node).includes(d))
      .style('fill', 'red');
  } else {
    // Unhighlight all nodes
    svg.selectAll('circle').style('fill', 'lightblue');
  }
});
```
This code creates an instance of the `Graph` class from the `graph-theory` library, and adds some sample nodes and edges to it. It then defines two layouts (`Spring Layout` and `Circular Layout`) and two algorithms (`Breadth-First Search` and `Depth-First Search`) that can be used to analyze and explore the graph.

The code also creates an interactive visualization of the graph using D3.js, with nodes represented as circles and edges represented as lines. The user can click on a node to highlight it and its neighbors, or click outside of any node to unhighlight all nodes.

Note that this is just sample code, and you will need to modify it to suit your specific needs. For example, you may want to add more layouts and algorithms, or change the styles for the nodes and edges. You can also use other libraries such as `d3-force` to create a more interactive visualization of the graph.

