```
// Import the graph theory library
const Graph = require('graph-theory');

// Create a new graph object
const graph = new Graph();

// Add nodes and edges to the graph
graph.addNode('A');
graph.addNode('B');
graph.addEdge('A', 'B');

// Define layouts for the graph
const layouts = {
  'default': {
    name: 'Default Layout',
    algorithm: 'spring'
  },
  'force-directed': {
    name: 'Force-Directed Layout',
    algorithm: 'force-directed'
  }
};

// Define algorithms for the graph
const algorithms = {
  'spring': {
    name: 'Spring Layout',
    options: {
      stiffness: 100,
      damping: 0.5
    }
  },
  'force-directed': {
    name: 'Force-Directed Layout',
    options: {
      iterations: 100,
      alpha: 0.5
    }
  }
};

// Create a function to update the graph layout
function updateLayout(layout) {
  // Update the layout of the graph using the selected algorithm
  const algorithm = algorithms[layout];
  graph.layout(algorithm);
}

// Create a function to render the graph
function renderGraph() {
  // Render the graph using the current layout and style
  const layout = layouts['default'];
  const algorithm = algorithms[layout.algorithm];
  graph.render(algorithm, layout.options);
}

// Add event listeners for user input
document.getElementById('layout-select').addEventListener('change', (event) => {
  updateLayout(event.target.value);
});

document.getElementById('render-button').addEventListener('click', () => {
  renderGraph();
});
```
This code creates a new graph object using the `graph-theory` library, and adds nodes and edges to it. It then defines two layouts for the graph: a default layout that uses the spring algorithm, and a force-directed layout that uses the force-directed algorithm. The code also defines two algorithms for the graph: the spring algorithm and the force-directed algorithm.

The `updateLayout` function is called when the user selects a new layout from the dropdown menu. It updates the layout of the graph using the selected algorithm, and renders the graph using the current style. The `renderGraph` function is called when the user clicks the "Render Graph" button. It renders the graph using the current layout and style.

The code also adds event listeners for user input: when the user selects a new layout from the dropdown menu, the `updateLayout` function is called to update the layout of the graph. When the user clicks the "Render Graph" button, the `renderGraph` function is called to render the graph using the current layout and style.

