```
import { useCallback } from 'react';
import { pureCall } from 'react-pure-call';

const MyComponent = () => {
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    // This method call is marked as pure, so it will be tree shaken
    // and only included in the production build if it's actually used.
    pureCall(myMethod);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>
    </div>
  );
};
```
In this example, we use the `useCallback` hook from React to memoize the `handleClick` method. We also import the `pureCall` function from the `react-pure-call` plugin, which allows us to mark top-level React method calls as pure for tree shaking.

When a method is marked as pure, it means that the method will not have any side effects and can be safely removed if it's not actually used in the code. This can help reduce the size of the production build by only including the necessary code.

In this case, we mark the `myMethod` method as pure using the `pureCall` function, which will allow React to tree shake it from the production build if it's not actually used.

Here is an example of how you can use the `react-pure-call` plugin to mark top-level React method calls as pure for tree shaking:
```
import { PureCall } from 'react-pure-call';

const MyComponent = () => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <p>{count}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default PureCall(MyComponent);
```
In this example, the `PureCall` function is used to wrap the `MyComponent` component. This will mark all top-level method calls within the component as pure, allowing for more efficient production builds by only including necessary code.

Note that you will need to install the `react-pure-call` package in order to use this plugin. You can do this using npm by running the following command:
```
npm install react-pure-call
```

