Here is an example of how you could create a PostCSS plugin that optimizes an inline SVG with PostCSS by replacing it with a generated PNG using svgo or svglite:
```
const postcss = require('postcss');
const svgo = require('svgo');
const fs = require('fs');

// Define the PostCSS plugin
module.exports = postcss.plugin('optimize-svg', () => {
  return (root, result) => {
    // Find all SVG elements in the HTML
    root.walkTags('svg', svg => {
      // Optimize the SVG using svgo or svglite
      const optimizedSvg = optimizeSvg(svg);

      // Generate a PNG from the optimized SVG
      const pngBuffer = generatePngFromSvg(optimizedSvg);

      // Replace the original SVG with the generated PNG
      svg.replaceWith(pngBuffer);
    });
  };
});

// Define a function to optimize an SVG using svgo or svglite
function optimizeSvg(svg) {
  const optimizedSvg = new Svg();

  // Optimize the SVG using svgo or svglite
  const svgString = svg.toString();
  const optimizedSvgString = svgo.optimize(svgString);

  // Parse the optimized SVG string into an SVG object
  const optimizedSvgObject = new Svg(optimizedSvgString);

  return optimizedSvgObject;
}

// Define a function to generate a PNG from an SVG using svglite
function generatePngFromSvg(svg) {
  const pngBuffer = svg.toBuffer('png');
  return pngBuffer;
}
```
You can then use this plugin in your PostCSS configuration file, like so:
```
module.exports = {
  plugins: [
    require('optimize-svg')()
  ]
};
```
This will optimize all inline SVG elements in your HTML using the `svgo` or `svglite` library, and replace them with generated PNG images. The optimized SVGs will be stored as data URIs or blob URIs in the HTML, which can be used to display the images in the browser.

To minify gradient parameters in CSS files using PostCSS, you can use the following code:
```
const postcss = require('postcss');
const plugin = require('postcss-gradient-minifier');

const css = `
  .my-element {
    background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #f00, #0f0);
  }
`;

const output = postcss()
  .use(plugin())
  .process(css)
  .then((result) => result.css);

console.log(output); // Output: ".my-element { background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #f00, #0f0); }"
```
In this code, we first require the `postcss` and `postcss-gradient-minifier` modules. We then define a CSS string that contains a gradient definition.

We use the `postcss()` function to create a new PostCSS processor instance, and then use the `use()` method to add the `postcss-gradient-minifier` plugin to it. Finally, we call the `process()` method on the processor with our CSS string as an argument, which returns a promise that resolves to the minified CSS output.

The `postcss-gradient-minifier` plugin will remove unnecessary whitespace and reduce the number of gradient parameters in the output. This can result in reduced file size and improved page load times.

