Here is an example of how you can implement the OpenTelemetry semantic conventions for logging, tracing, and metrics in a Node.js application using the OpenTelemetry JavaScript library:
```
const { Tracer } = require('@opentelemetry/tracing');
const { Meter } = require('@opentelemetry/metrics');
const { Logger } = require('@opentelemetry/logging');

// Create a tracer and meter instance
const tracer = new Tracer();
const meter = new Meter();

// Create a logger instance
const logger = new Logger();

// Define the log level for the logger
logger.setLevel('info');

// Define the tracing configuration
tracer.configure({
  // Set the service name for the tracer
  serviceName: 'my-service',

  // Set the sampling rate for the tracer
  samplingRate: 0.5,

  // Set the reporting interval for the tracer
  reportingInterval: 1000,
});

// Define the metrics configuration
meter.configure({
  // Set the service name for the meter
  serviceName: 'my-service',

  // Set the reporting interval for the meter
  reportingInterval: 60000,
});

// Create a span for the current request
const span = tracer.startSpan('my-span');

// Add attributes to the span
span.setAttribute('key1', 'value1');
span.setAttribute('key2', 'value2');

// Log a message with the logger
logger.info('Hello, world!');

// Record a metric for the current request
meter.record({
  name: 'my-metric',
  value: 10,
});

// End the span
span.end();
```
This code creates a tracer and meter instance using the OpenTelemetry JavaScript library, and defines the log level for the logger to be `info`. It also sets the service name for both the tracer and meter to `my-service`, and configures the sampling rate and reporting interval for the tracer. The code then creates a span for the current request using the `startSpan` method of the tracer, adds attributes to the span using the `setAttribute` method, logs a message with the logger using the `info` method, records a metric for the current request using the `record` method of the meter, and ends the span using the `end` method.

Note that this is just an example, and you will need to modify it to fit your specific use case. Additionally, you may want to consider using a more robust logging library than the one provided by OpenTelemetry, as well as configuring your tracer and meter instances with appropriate settings for your application.

