To treat React JSX elements as value types and hoist them to the highest scope, you can use a library like `react-hoist`. This library provides a simple API for hoisting React components to the highest scope in your application.

Here's an example of how you can use `react-hoist` to hoist a React component to the highest scope:
```
import { hoist } from 'react-hoist';

const MyComponent = () => (
  <div>Hello, world!</div>
);

export default hoist(MyComponent);
```
In this example, we define a React component called `MyComponent` that returns a JSX element. We then export the component using the `hoist` function from `react-hoist`. This will hoist the component to the highest scope in your application, making it available throughout the entire app.

Alternatively, you can implement a custom solution using JavaScript prototypes and scoping rules. Here's an example of how you can do this:
```
const MyComponent = () => (
  <div>Hello, world!</div>
);

MyComponent.prototype.hoist = function() {
  return this;
};

export default MyComponent.prototype.hoist();
```
In this example, we define a React component called `MyComponent` that returns a JSX element. We then add a `hoist` method to the prototype of the component using the `prototype` property. This method simply returns the component itself, which allows us to hoist it to the highest scope in our application.

Finally, we export the component using the `export default` statement, which will make it available throughout the entire app.

