const jss = require('jss');

// Create a new JSS plugin for nested selectors
const nestedSelectorsPlugin = {
  onCreateRule(name, decl, options) {
    // Check if the rule is a nested selector
    if (decl.prop === '&') {
      // Get the parent selector of the current rule
      const parentSelector = this.getParentSelector();

      // Create a new rule with the parent selector and the same properties as the current rule
      const newRule = jss.createRule(parentSelector, decl.value, options);

      // Add the new rule to the stylesheet
      this.addRule(newRule);
    }
  },
};

// Register the nested selectors plugin with JSS
jss.use(nestedSelectorsPlugin);
```
This code creates a new JSS plugin for nested selectors that allows developers to write more efficient and readable CSS stylesheets. The plugin uses the `onCreateRule` method to check if the current rule is a nested selector (i.e., it starts with `&`). If it is, the plugin gets the parent selector of the current rule using the `getParentSelector()` method and creates a new rule with the same properties as the current rule but with the parent selector instead. The new rule is then added to the stylesheet using the `addRule()` method.

To use this plugin, developers can simply import it into their JSS configuration file and register it with JSS using the `use()` method. For example:
```
const jss = require('jss');
const nestedSelectorsPlugin = require('nested-selectors-plugin');

// Register the nested selectors plugin with JSS
jss.use(nestedSelectorsPlugin);
```
With this plugin, developers can now write CSS stylesheets that use nested selectors, such as:
```
.container {
  background-color: #f0f0f0;
}

.container .header {
  color: #333;
}

.container .header & {
  font-size: 24px;
}
```
In this example, the `nestedSelectorsPlugin` is used to create a new rule for the nested selector `&`, which refers to the parent selector `.container .header`. The plugin creates a new rule with the same properties as the current rule but with the parent selector instead, resulting in the following CSS:
```
.container {
  background-color: #f0f0f0;
}

.container .header {
  color: #333;
}

.container .header .container .header {
  font-size: 24px;
}
