```
import React from 'react';
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';
import { loadReCaptcha } from '@google/recaptcha';

class ReCaptcha extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = {
      loaded: false,
      token: null,
    };
  }

  componentDidMount() {
    loadReCaptcha().then((recaptcha) => {
      this.setState({
        loaded: true,
        recaptcha,
      });
    });
  }

  render() {
    if (!this.state.loaded) return null;

    const { sitekey, onVerify } = this.props;
    const { token } = this.state;

    return (
      <div>
        <div id="recaptcha" />
        <button onClick={() => this.setState({ token: recaptcha.getResponse() })}>
          Verify
        </button>
        {token && onVerify(token) && <p>Token verified</p>}
      </div>
    );
  }
}

ReCaptcha.propTypes = {
  sitekey: PropTypes.string.isRequired,
  onVerify: PropTypes.func.isRequired,
};

export default ReCaptcha;
```
This code creates a React component wrapper for Google reCAPTCHA that allows developers to easily integrate reCAPTCHA into their React applications with minimal setup and configuration. The `ReCaptcha` component takes in two props: `sitekey` and `onVerify`. The `sitekey` prop is used to set the site key for the reCAPTCHA instance, while the `onVerify` prop is a callback function that will be called with the token returned by the reCAPTCHA instance when the user verifies themselves.

The component uses the `@google/recaptcha` library to load the reCAPTCHA script and create an instance of the reCAPTCHA widget. The `componentDidMount` lifecycle method is used to initialize the reCAPTCHA instance and set up the callback function for when the user verifies themselves.

The component renders a button that, when clicked, calls the `getResponse` method on the reCAPTCHA instance to retrieve the token and updates the state with the new token value. The component also renders a paragraph element that displays the message "Token verified" if the token is not null and the `onVerify` callback function returns true.

To use this component in your React application, you can import it and pass in the necessary props:
```
import ReCaptcha from './ReCaptcha';

function MyComponent() {
  return (
    <div>
      <ReCaptcha sitekey="your-site-key" onVerify={(token) => console.log(token)} />
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, the `MyComponent` component imports the `ReCaptcha` component and passes in a site key and an `onVerify` callback function that logs the token to the console. The `ReCaptcha` component will then render the reCAPTCHA widget and display the button and paragraph elements when the user verifies themselves.

