Here is an example of how you could create a comprehensive material component framework for Vue using the Material Design guidelines as a reference:
```
// Import necessary dependencies
import { Component } from 'vue';
import { MatButton, MatIcon, MatTooltip } from '@material/vue';

// Define customizable components
const CustomButton = {
  extends: MatButton,
  props: {
    icon: String,
    label: String,
  },
};

const CustomIcon = {
  extends: MatIcon,
  props: {
    name: String,
    size: Number,
  },
};

// Define animations
const FadeInAnimation = {
  from: { opacity: 0 },
  to: { opacity: 1 },
  duration: 500,
};

const SlideUpAnimation = {
  from: { transform: 'translateY(100%)' },
  to: { transform: 'translateY(0)' },
  duration: 500,
};

// Define icons
const CustomIconSet = {
  name: 'custom-icons',
  icons: [
    { name: 'home', path: '/assets/icons/home.svg' },
    { name: 'search', path: '/assets/icons/search.svg' },
    { name: 'settings', path: '/assets/icons/settings.svg' },
  ],
};

// Define component tree
const App = {
  components: {
    CustomButton,
    CustomIcon,
    FadeInAnimation,
    SlideUpAnimation,
    CustomIconSet,
  },
  template: `
    <div>
      <custom-button icon="home" label="Home"></custom-button>
      <custom-icon name="search" size="24px"></custom-icon>
      <fade-in-animation></fade-in-animation>
      <slide-up-animation></slide-up-animation>
    </div>
  `,
};
```
This code defines a customizable button component using the Material Design guidelines as a reference, and also includes animations and icons. The `CustomButton` component extends the `MatButton` component and adds two props: `icon` and `label`. The `CustomIcon` component extends the `MatIcon` component and adds two props: `name` and `size`.

The `FadeInAnimation` and `SlideUpAnimation` components are defined as animations that can be used to add a fade-in or slide-up effect to elements. The `CustomIconSet` component defines a set of custom icons that can be used in the application.

Finally, the `App` component is defined as the root component of the application and includes instances of the customizable button, icon, animation, and icon components. The template for the `App` component includes placeholders for the customizable components and animations.

This code uses best practices for performance optimization and accessibility by using the Material Design guidelines as a reference for creating customizable components and animations, and also by using the `MatButton`, `MatIcon`, and `MatTooltip` components from the `@material/vue` library to create a comprehensive material component framework.

Here is an example of how you could create a library of responsive and accessible React UI components using React and Emotion:
```
import React from 'react';
import { ThemeProvider } from '@emotion/react';
import { useMediaQuery } from '@emotion/react-media-query';

const theme = {
  colors: {
    primary: '#3498db',
    secondary: '#f1c40f',
    error: '#e74c3c'
  },
  breakpoints: {
    sm: '(min-width: 600px)',
    md: '(min-width: 960px)',
    lg: '(min-width: 1280px)'
  }
};

const NavigationBar = ({ children }) => {
  const isSmallScreen = useMediaQuery('(max-width: 600px)');
  return (
    <ThemeProvider theme={theme}>
      <nav>
        {isSmallScreen ? (
          <ul>{children}</ul>
        ) : (
          <ol>{children}</ol>
        )}
      </nav>
    </ThemeProvider>
  );
};

const Tab = ({ children }) => {
  const isActive = useMediaQuery('(min-width: 600px)');
  return (
    <ThemeProvider theme={theme}>
      <li className={isActive ? 'active' : ''}>{children}</li>
    </ThemeProvider>
  );
};

const Form = ({ children }) => {
  const isSmallScreen = useMediaQuery('(max-width: 600px)');
  return (
    <ThemeProvider theme={theme}>
      <form className={isSmallScreen ? 'small' : ''}>{children}</form>
    </ThemeProvider>
  );
};

const Alert = ({ children }) => {
  const isActive = useMediaQuery('(min-width: 600px)');
  return (
    <ThemeProvider theme={theme}>
      <div className={isActive ? 'active' : ''}>{children}</div>
    </ThemeProvider>
  );
};

const LoadingIndicator = () => {
  const isSmallScreen = useMediaQuery('(max-width: 600px)');
  return (
    <ThemeProvider theme={theme}>
      <div className={isSmallScreen ? 'small' : ''}>Loading...</div>
    </ThemeProvider>
  );
};
```
This code defines a library of responsive and accessible React UI components using React and Emotion. The `NavigationBar`, `Tab`, `Form`, `Alert`, and `LoadingIndicator` components are all customizable through the use of themes and media queries. The `ThemeProvider` component is used to provide the theme to the child components, and the `useMediaQuery` hook is used to determine the current screen size and apply appropriate styles based on that.

You can use these components in your application like this:
```
import React from 'react';
import { NavigationBar, Tab, Form, Alert, LoadingIndicator } from './components';

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <NavigationBar>
        <Tab>Home</Tab>
        <Tab>About</Tab>
        <Tab>Contact</Tab>
      </NavigationBar>
      <Form>
        <label htmlFor="name">Name:</label>
        <input type="text" id="name" />
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
      </Form>
      <Alert>This is an alert message!</Alert>
      <LoadingIndicator />
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, the `NavigationBar` component is used to create a navigation bar with three tabs. The `Tab` component is used to create each tab, and the `Form` component is used to create a form with a label, input field, and submit button. The `Alert` component is used to create an alert message, and the `LoadingIndicator` component is used to display a loading indicator while data is being loaded.

You can customize the appearance of these components by providing your own theme object or by using media queries to apply different styles based on the current screen size.

```
import { Platform } from 'react-native';

const NetworkInfo = {
  getCurrentWifiSignalStrength: () => {
    if (Platform.OS === 'ios') {
      // iOS implementation
      return fetch('https://api.example.com/wifi_signal_strength')
        .then(response => response.json())
        .catch(error => console.log(error));
    } else if (Platform.OS === 'android') {
      // Android implementation
      const wifiManager = require('@react-native-community/wifi-manager');
      return wifiManager.getCurrentWifiSignalStrength();
    }
  },
  getCellularDataUsage: () => {
    if (Platform.OS === 'ios') {
      // iOS implementation
      const network = require('react-native-networking');
      return network.getCellularDataUsage();
    } else if (Platform.OS === 'android') {
      // Android implementation
      const telephonyManager = require('@react-native-community/telephony-manager');
      return telephonyManager.getCellularDataUsage();
    }
  },
  getDeviceIPAddress: () => {
    if (Platform.OS === 'ios') {
      // iOS implementation
      const network = require('react-native-networking');
      return network.getIPAddress();
    } else if (Platform.OS === 'android') {
      // Android implementation
      const wifiManager = require('@react-native-community/wifi-manager');
      return wifiManager.getDeviceIPAddress();
    }
  },
};

export default NetworkInfo;
```
This code creates a React Native module called `NetworkInfo` that provides an easy-to-use interface for accessing network information on both iOS and Android platforms. The module exposes three functions: `getCurrentWifiSignalStrength`, `getCellularDataUsage`, and `getDeviceIPAddress`. These functions return the current WiFi signal strength, cellular data usage, and device IP address respectively.

The implementation of these functions is platform-specific, meaning that they use different APIs on iOS and Android to retrieve the network information. On iOS, the `react-native-networking` module is used to get the WiFi signal strength and device IP address. On Android, the `@react-native-community/wifi-manager` and `@react-native-community/telephony-manager` modules are used to get the WiFi signal strength and cellular data usage respectively.

The `NetworkInfo` module is exported as a default export, making it easy to import and use in other parts of the React Native application.

```
const fs = require('fs');
const path = require('path');
const vueCliApi = require('@vue/cli-api');

// Set up the Vue CLI API
const api = new vueCliApi();

// Define the directory to parse for component files
const componentsDir = 'src/components';

// Loop through each file in the components directory and extract information
fs.readdirSync(componentsDir).forEach((file) => {
  // Get the full path of the current file
  const filePath = path.join(componentsDir, file);

  // Parse the Vue component file using the Vue CLI API
  const parsedComponent = api.parseComponent(filePath);

  // Extract the component name and template HTML
  const componentName = parsedComponent.name;
  const templateHtml = parsedComponent.template;

  // Extract any exported props or methods
  const props = parsedComponent.props || [];
  const methods = parsedComponent.methods || [];

  // Output the extracted data in a format suitable for documentation generation
  console.log(`${componentName}:`);
  console.log(`  Template: ${templateHtml}`);
  console.log(`  Props:`);
  props.forEach((prop) => {
    console.log(`    - ${prop.name} (${prop.type})`);
  });
  console.log(`  Methods:`);
  methods.forEach((method) => {
    console.log(`    - ${method.name} (${method.type})`);
  });
});
```
This code uses the `fs` and `path` modules to read the contents of a directory, and the `@vue/cli-api` module to parse Vue component files using the Vue CLI API. It then extracts information such as the component name, template HTML, and any exported props or methods, and outputs the extracted data in a format suitable for documentation generation.

Note that this code assumes that the Vue component files are located in a directory named `src/components`. You may need to modify the path to match your specific project structure. Additionally, this code only extracts information from the first level of nested components, so if you have nested components within other components, you will need to modify the code accordingly.

