1. To create an array of all the files in the current directory, you can use the `fs` module's `readdirSync()` method to read the contents of the directory and then filter out any non-file entries:
```
const fs = require('fs');
const dirContents = fs.readdirSync('.').filter(entry => entry.isFile());
console.log(dirContents);
```
This will output an array of file names in the current directory.

2. To create an object that maps each file name to its corresponding file contents, you can use the `fs` module's `readFileSync()` method to read the contents of each file and then add it to the object:
```
const fs = require('fs');
const files = {};
fs.readdirSync('.').forEach(file => {
  const content = fs.readFileSync(file);
  files[file] = content;
});
console.log(files);
```
This will output an object with the file names as keys and their corresponding contents as values.

3. To create a new file with a specified name and write the contents of the file to it, you can use the `fs` module's `writeFileSync()` method:
```
const fs = require('fs');
const fileName = 'new_file.txt';
const content = 'Hello, world!';
fs.writeFileSync(fileName, content);
console.log(`Created file ${fileName} with contents ${content}`);
```
This will create a new file named `new_file.txt` in the current directory and write the string `'Hello, world!'` to it.

4. To read the contents of a file and return the content as a string, you can use the `fs` module's `readFileSync()` method:
```
const fs = require('fs');
const fileName = 'existing_file.txt';
const content = fs.readFileSync(fileName);
console.log(`Read file ${fileName} with contents ${content}`);
```
This will read the contents of a file named `existing_file.txt` in the current directory and output its contents to the console.

5. To generate a minified bundle that includes all the files, you can use the `fs` module's `readdirSync()` method to read the contents of the directory and then concatenate the file contents:
```
const fs = require('fs');
const dirContents = fs.readdirSync('.').filter(entry => entry.isFile());
let bundle = '';
dirContents.forEach(file => {
  const content = fs.readFileSync(file);
  bundle += content;
});
console.log(`Generated minified bundle with contents ${bundle}`);
```
This will output a string that contains the concatenated contents of all the files in the current directory.

6. To generate a minified bundle that includes all the files and also includes a reference to a specific library, you can use the `fs` module's `readdirSync()` method to read the contents of the directory and then concatenate the file contents:
```
const fs = require('fs');
const dirContents = fs.readdirSync('.').filter(entry => entry.isFile());
let bundle = '';
dirContents.forEach(file => {
  const content = fs.readFileSync(file);
  bundle += content;
});
bundle += `var library = require('library');`;
console.log(`Generated minified bundle with contents ${bundle}`);
```
This will output a string that contains the concatenated contents of all the files in the current directory, followed by a reference to a specific library.

7. To generate a minified bundle that includes all the files and also includes a reference to a specific library and also adds a timestamp to the file name, you can use the `fs` module's `readdirSync()` method to read the contents of the directory and then concatenate the file contents:
```
const fs = require('fs');
const dirContents = fs.readdirSync('.').filter(entry => entry.isFile());
let bundle = '';
dirContents.forEach(file => {
  const content = fs.readFileSync(file);
  bundle += content;
});
bundle += `var library = require('library');`;
const timestamp = new Date().toISOString();
bundle += `// ${timestamp}`;
console.log(`Generated minified bundle with contents ${bundle}`);
```
This will output a string that contains the concatenated contents of all the files in the current directory, followed by a reference to a specific library and a timestamp added to the file name.

