```
const postcss = require('postcss');
const sassVariables = require('sass-variables');

module.exports = postcss.plugin('sass-like-variables', () => {
  return (root, result) => {
    root.walkDecls(decl => {
      const value = decl.value;
      if (value.includes('$')) {
        const variableName = value.match(/^\$(.*)$/)[1];
        const variableValue = sassVariables[variableName];
        if (variableValue) {
          decl.value = decl.value.replace(/\$\w+/, variableValue);
        }
      }
    });
  };
});
```
This code defines a PostCSS plugin that enables Sass-like variables in CSS code. The plugin uses the `sass-variables` library to look up the value of each variable reference and replaces it with the actual value in the CSS declaration. For example, if a developer defines a variable `$color` with the value `#ff0000`, any occurrence of `$color` in a CSS declaration will be replaced with the actual color value `#ff0000`.

To use this plugin, developers can simply import it and add it to their PostCSS configuration. For example:
```
const postcss = require('postcss');
const sassLikeVariables = require('sass-like-variables');

const css = `
  .button {
    background-color: $color;
  }
`;

const output = postcss()
  .use(sassLikeVariables)
  .process(css);
```
In this example, the plugin will be applied to the CSS code and any variable references will be replaced with their actual values. The resulting CSS code will be:
```
.button {
  background-color: #ff0000;
}
