```
const { Linter } = require('eslint');
const tsDoc = require('ts-doc');

class TypeScriptDocLinter extends Linter {
  constructor(context) {
    super(context);
    this.parserServices = context.parserServices;
  }

  lint(node, syntaxError) {
    const docComment = tsDoc.parse(node.leadingComments);
    if (docComment.tags.length > 0) {
      // Check for any errors or warnings in the doc comment tags
      const errors = this.checkTags(docComment.tags);
      if (errors.length > 0) {
        return errors;
      }
    }
    return [];
  }

  checkTags(tags) {
    // Implement your custom logic to check the doc comment tags for errors or warnings
    // For example, you could check that all required tags are present and have valid values
    const errors = [];
    for (const tag of tags) {
      if (tag.name === 'required' && !tag.value) {
        errors.push({
          message: `The @${tag.name} tag is missing a value`,
          line: node.loc.start.line,
          column: node.loc.start.column + 1, // Adjust for the '@' character
        });
      }
    }
    return errors;
  }
}

module.exports = {
  create(context) {
    const linter = new TypeScriptDocLinter(context);
    return {
      Program(node) {
        linter.lint(node, null);
      },
    };
  },
};
```
This code creates an ESLint plugin that validates TypeScript doc comments by parsing the comments using the `ts-doc` library and then utilizing ESLint's API to report any errors or warnings based on the parsed information. The plugin uses the `Linter` class from ESLint to create a custom linter that can be used with any ESLint rule.

The `TypeScriptDocLinter` class extends the `Linter` class and overrides the `lint` method to check for errors or warnings in the doc comment tags. The `checkTags` method is called from within the `lint` method to perform the actual validation of the doc comments.

In this example, we assume that the `ts-doc` library has already been installed and imported into the plugin. We also assume that the plugin is being used with a TypeScript project, so the `Program` node passed to the linter will be a TypeScript program node.

The `create` method of the plugin returns an object with a single property, `Program`, which is a function that takes a `node` argument and calls the `lint` method on the `TypeScriptDocLinter` instance. This allows the plugin to be used with any ESLint rule that expects a linter function as its argument.

