To load multiple models on the same route in EmberJS, you can use the `setupController` hook in your route. This hook allows you to set up any data that needs to be passed to the template.

Here is an example of how you could use `setupController` to load multiple models:
```
import Route from '@ember/routing/route';
import { inject as service } from '@ember/service';

export default class MyRoute extends Route {
  @service store;

  model() {
    return this.store.findAll('model1');
  }

  setupController(controller, model) {
    controller.set('model1', model);
    controller.set('model2', this.store.findAll('model2'));
  }
}
```
In this example, the `model` hook returns a promise that resolves to an array of all instances of the `model1` model. The `setupController` hook is then called with the resolved value of the `model` hook and the controller instance as arguments. In this method, we set the `model1` property on the controller to the resolved value of the `model` hook, and also set the `model2` property to an array of all instances of the `model2` model using the `store.findAll` method.

It's important to note that the `setupController` hook is only called once per route transition, so if you need to update the data in your controller based on user input or other events, you should use a different approach.

You can also use the `afterModel` hook to load multiple models and set them as properties of the controller. Here's an example:
```
import Route from '@ember/routing/route';
import { inject as service } from '@ember/service';

export default class MyRoute extends Route {
  @service store;

  afterModel(resolvedModels) {
    const model1 = resolvedModels.model1;
    const model2 = resolvedModels.model2;
    this.controllerFor('my-route').setProperties({
      model1,
      model2
    });
  }
}
```
In this example, the `afterModel` hook is called with an object that contains the resolved values of the `model` and `setupController` hooks as properties. We then use the `controllerFor` method to get a reference to the controller for the current route, and set the `model1` and `model2` properties on the controller using the `setProperties` method.

It's important to note that the `afterModel` hook is called every time the route is entered, so if you need to update the data in your controller based on user input or other events, you should use a different approach.

