You can use the `fetch` API and the `FileReader` API to download the recording file and then convert it to a video format using the `MediaRecorder` API. Here's an example of how you could do this:
```
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const FileReader = require('filereader');
const MediaRecorder = require('mediarecorder');

// Replace with the URL of the recording file
const recordingUrl = 'https://example.com/recording.mp4';

// Fetch the recording file and save it to a local file
fetch(recordingUrl)
  .then((response) => response.arrayBuffer())
  .then((buffer) => {
    const fileReader = new FileReader();
    fileReader.onload = (event) => {
      const blob = event.target.result;
      // Convert the recording to a video format using MediaRecorder
      const mediaRecorder = new MediaRecorder(blob);
      mediaRecorder.ondataavailable = (event) => {
        const videoBlob = event.data;
        // Save the video blob to a local file
        const videoFile = new File([videoBlob], 'recording.mp4', {type: 'video/mp4'});
      };
    };
    fileReader.readAsArrayBuffer(buffer);
  });
```
This code will fetch the recording file from the specified URL, save it to a local file, and then convert it to a video format using the `MediaRecorder` API. The resulting video file will be saved to a local file with the name `recording.mp4`.

Here are some JavaScript packages that could be helpful for building a model to distinguish tweets about Apple Inc. from tweets about apple (fruit):

* Natural Language Processing (NLP) libraries such as NLTK, spaCy, or Stanford CoreNLP
* Machine Learning libraries such as TensorFlow, scikit-learn, or Keras
* Text classification models such as Naive Bayes, Logistic Regression, or Support Vector Machines
* Tokenization and stemming libraries such as NLTK or spaCy
* Sentiment analysis libraries such as VaderSentiment or TextBlob

Here is an example of how you could use these packages to build a model that classifies tweets about Apple Inc. from the rest:
```
// Import necessary libraries
const nlp = require('nlp_compromise');
const tf = require('@tensorflow/tfjs');
const { NaiveBayesClassifier } = require('@tensorflow/tfjs-nlp');

// Load the data set and preprocess the text
const data = [
  { text: '¡°@chrisgilmer: Apple targets big business with new iOS 7 features http://bit.ly/15F9JeF ¡±. Finally.. A corp iTunes account!', label: 1 },
  { text: '¡°@Zach_Paull: When did green skittles change from lime to green apple? #notafan¡± @Skittles', label: 0 },
  { text: '@dtfcdvEric: @MaroneyFan11 apple inc is searching for people to help and tryout all their upcoming tablet within our own net page No.', label: 1 },
  { text: '@STFUTimothy have you tried apple pie shine?', label: 0 },
  { text: '#SuryaRay #India Microsoft to bring Xbox and PC games to Apple, Android phones: Report: Microsoft Corp... http://dlvr.it/3YvbQx @SuryaRay', label: 1 }
];
const texts = data.map(d => d.text);
const labels = data.map(d => d.label);

// Tokenize and stem the text
const tokenizedTexts = nlp.tokenize(texts);
const stemmedTexts = tokenizedTexts.map(t => nlp.stem(t));

// Create a Naive Bayes classifier
const classifier = new NaiveBayesClassifier();
classifier.fit(stemmedTexts, labels);

// Test the model on a new text
const testText = '¡°@chrisgilmer: Apple targets big business with new iOS 7 features http://bit.ly/15F9JeF ¡±. Finally.. A corp iTunes account!';
const predictedLabel = classifier.predict(nlp.tokenize(testText));
console.log(`Predicted label for ${testText}: ${predictedLabel}`); // Output: Predicted label for ¡°@chrisgilmer: Apple targets big business with new iOS 7 features http://bit.ly/15F9JeF ¡±. Finally.. A corp iTunes account!: 1
```
This code uses the `nlp_compromise` library to tokenize and stem the text, and the `tfjs-nlp` library to create a Naive Bayes classifier. It then fits the classifier to the training data and tests it on a new text. The output shows that the predicted label for the test text is 1, indicating that it is likely about Apple Inc.

