To implement infinite scrolling in an Ember.js application using lazy loading, you can use the `infinite-scroll` addon. This addon provides a simple way to load more data as the user scrolls down the page.

Here's an example of how you can use it:
```
// app/templates/my-template.hbs
<div class="infinite-scroll">
  {{#each model as |item|}}
    <div>{{item.name}}</div>
  {{/each}}
  <div class="infinite-scroll-loading">Loading...</div>
</div>
```

```
// app/routes/my-route.js
import Route from '@ember/routing/route';
import InfiniteScrollMixin from 'ember-infinite-scroll/mixins/infinite-scroll';

export default Route.extend(InfiniteScrollMixin, {
  model() {
    return this.store.findAll('my-model');
  },

  infiniteScroll: function() {
    // Load more data when the user scrolls down
    const page = this.get('page') || 1;
    this.set('page', page + 1);
    return this.store.findAll('my-model', { page });
  }
});
```
In the above example, we're using the `infiniteScroll` mixin to load more data as the user scrolls down the page. The `infiniteScroll` method is called whenever the user scrolls down and it loads more data from the server. We're also keeping track of the current page number using the `page` property.

You can customize the loading behavior by overriding the `infiniteScroll` method and providing your own implementation. For example, you can use a different endpoint to load more data or you can use a different strategy for pagination.

It's also important to note that this addon uses the `scroll` event of the window object to detect when the user has scrolled down the page. If you want to use a different event, you can specify it using the `eventName` property of the mixin.
