response_prompt:
You can use the `marked` package to parse Markdown text and convert it into HTML tags. Here's an example of how you can use it in your Nuxt3 application:
```js
import { marked } from 'marked';

const Services = await $directus.items('Services').readByQuery();
const htmlContent = marked(Services.data.Test[0].Title);
console.log(htmlContent);
```
This will parse the Markdown text in `Services.data.Test[0].Title` and convert it into HTML tags, which you can then use to display the content in your Nuxt3 application.

Alternatively, you can also use the `marked-it` package, which is a more powerful version of the `marked` package that allows you to customize the parsing options and add additional features such as syntax highlighting and emoji support. Here's an example of how you can use it in your Nuxt3 application:
```js
import { marked } from 'marked-it';

const Services = await $directus.items('Services').readByQuery();
const htmlContent = marked(Services.data.Test[0].Title, {
  gfm: true,
  breaks: true,
});
console.log(htmlContent);
```
This will parse the Markdown text in `Services.data.Test[0].Title` and convert it into HTML tags using the `gfm` (GitHub Flavored Markdown) parser, which allows you to use GitHub-style Markdown syntax. The `breaks` option is set to `true`, which will allow you to use line breaks in your Markdown text.

You can then use the `htmlContent` variable to display the parsed HTML content in your Nuxt3 application.

