response_prompt:
The issue you are facing is likely due to the fact that the `process.env` object in your Expo project is not populated with the environment variables defined in your `.env` file. This is because Expo uses a different mechanism for loading environment variables, which is based on the `expo-config` package.

To fix this issue, you can try the following:

1. Add the `expo-config` package to your project by running `npm install expo-config` or `yarn add expo-config`.
2. In your `App.js` file, import the `expo-config` package and use it to load the environment variables defined in your `.env` file:
```javascript
import { StatusBar } from "expo-status-bar";
import { StyleSheet, Text, View } from "react-native";
import * as ExpoConfig from "expo-config";

export default function App() {
  const env = ExpoConfig.load();
  console.log(env); // This should now print the environment variables defined in your .env file
  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Text>Hello World!</Text>
    </View>
  );
}
```
Note that this will only work if you have defined the environment variables in your `.env` file and have run `expo prebuild` or `expo build` to generate the native code for your project.

I believe the issue you are experiencing is due to the fact that the `onDragEnd` event is being triggered twice when swapping distant items. This is because the `Sortable` component is trying to reposition the item back to its original position, which causes the animation to run again.

To fix this issue, you can try adding a check in your `onDragEnd` function to see if the item has already been moved and skip the animation if it has. Here's an example of how you could modify your code:
```
import { Sortable } from 'react-dnd-kit';

const MySortable = () => {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([
    { id: 1, name: 'Item 1' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Item 2' },
    { id: 3, name: 'Item 3' },
  ]);

  const onDragEnd = (item, event) => {
    if (!event.isSwap) return; // Skip animation if item has already been moved

    setItems((prevItems) => {
      const newItems = [...prevItems];
      const oldIndex = newItems.findIndex((i) => i.id === item.id);
      const newIndex = event.newIndex;

      if (oldIndex !== newIndex) {
        const temp = newItems[oldIndex];
        newItems[oldIndex] = newItems[newIndex];
        newItems[newIndex] = temp;
      }

      return newItems;
    });
  };

  return (
    <Sortable items={items} onDragEnd={onDragEnd}>
      {(item) => (
        <div style={{ backgroundColor: 'blue', padding: '10px' }}>
          {item.name}
        </div>
      )}
    </Sortable>
  );
};
```
In this example, we added a check in the `onDragEnd` function to see if the item has already been moved by checking if the `isSwap` property is true. If it is, we skip the animation and return early. This should prevent the animation from running twice when swapping distant items.

