You can use the `fetch` API in the browser to send log data to GCP Cloud Logging. Here is an example of how you can do this:
```
const logData = {
  message: 'Hello, world!',
  severity: 'INFO'
};

fetch('https://logging.googleapis.com/v2/entries:write', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json'
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(logData)
})
.then(response => response.json())
.catch(error => console.error('Error sending log data to GCP Cloud Logging:', error));
```
This code sends a POST request to the `entries:write` endpoint of the GCP Cloud Logging API, with the log data in the body of the request. The `Content-Type` header is set to `application/json`, which tells the server that the request body contains JSON data.

You can also use the `gcp-logging` library, which provides a simple and easy-to-use API for logging from the browser. Here is an example of how you can use it:
```
const logData = {
  message: 'Hello, world!',
  severity: 'INFO'
};

gcpLogging.write(logData)
.then(() => console.log('Log data sent to GCP Cloud Logging'))
.catch(error => console.error('Error sending log data to GCP Cloud Logging:', error));
```
This code uses the `gcp-logging` library to send a log entry with the specified message and severity level to GCP Cloud Logging. The `write` method returns a promise that resolves when the log entry is successfully sent, or rejects with an error if there was a problem sending the log data.

Note that you will need to have the `gcp-logging` library installed in your project before you can use it. You can install it using npm by running the following command:
```
npm install gcp-logging
```

You can use the `gcp-logging` package to log to GCP Cloud Logging from the browser. This package provides a simple API for logging messages to Google Cloud Logging.

Here is an example of how you can use this package in your client-side code:
```
import { Logger } from 'gcp-logging';

const logger = new Logger('my-app');
logger.log('Hello, world!');
```
This will log the message "Hello, world!" to Google Cloud Logging with the name of the logger set to "my-app". You can also use other methods such as `info()`, `warn()`, and `error()` to log different types of messages.

Note that this package requires a valid GCP project ID and service account credentials to be set up in your browser's environment variables or configuration files. You can find more information on how to set these up in the `gcp-logging` documentation.

