response_prompt:
The issue you're experiencing is likely due to a compatibility problem between the version of Firefox you are using and the WebSocket protocol used by SignalR in your Blazor Server app.

SignalR uses WebSockets as its default transport, but it also supports other transports such as Long Polling or Forever Frame. However, some older versions of Firefox may not support WebSockets natively, which can cause issues with the SignalR connection.

To fix this issue, you can try a few things:

1. Upgrade to the latest version of Firefox. This should resolve any compatibility issues and allow your app to work properly.
2. Disable WebSockets in your Blazor Server app. You can do this by setting the `WebSocketOptions.Enabled` property to `false` in your `Startup.cs` file, like this:
```csharp
services.AddSignalR(options =>
{
    options.WebSocketOptions.Enabled = false;
});
```
This will force SignalR to use Long Polling or Forever Frame as its transport, which may work better with older versions of Firefox.
3. Use a different WebSocket library that is compatible with your version of Firefox. There are several libraries available that can help you achieve this, such as the `WebSocket-Node` library. You can install it using npm by running the following command:
```bash
npm install websocket-node
```
Then, in your `Startup.cs` file, you can use it like this:
```csharp
services.AddSignalR(options =>
{
    options.WebSocketOptions.Enabled = true;
    options.WebSocketOptions.Library = "websocket-node";
});
```
This will tell SignalR to use the `websocket-node` library as its WebSocket transport, which should work better with older versions of Firefox.

I hope one of these solutions helps you resolve your issue!

