2. Use the `ws` package's built-in failsafe to check if a port is already in use before trying to start your WebSocket server. You can do this by adding the following code to your `configureServer` method:
```typescript
const httpServer = server.httpServer;
const webSocketServer = new WebSocketServer({ noServer: true });

webSocketServer.on("connection", (socket, request) => {
  socket.on("message", (data, isBinary) => {
    console.log(`Recieved ${data}`);
  });

  socket.send("hi c:");
});

httpServer.on("upgrade", (request: any, socket: any, head: any) => {
  webSocketServer.handleUpgrade(request, socket, head, socket => {
    webSocketServer.emit("connection", socket, request);
  });
});

// Check if port is already in use before starting the WebSocket server
if (httpServer.listening) {
  httpServer.close();
}

httpServer.listen(8080);
```
This code checks if `httpServer` is already listening on a port using the `listening` property, and if it is, it closes the server before starting it again with the new WebSocket server. This should help avoid conflicts with Vite's dev server.

Alternatively, you can try using a different Vite plugin that allows you to create a WebSocket server without having to use `ws`. For example, you can use the `vite-plugin-websocket` package, which provides a simple way to create a WebSocket server in your Vite project.

