response_prompt:
Here are some JavaScript packages that may be helpful for solving the problem of grouping different timeframes in Highcharts:

* highcharts-data-grouping: This package provides data grouping functionality for Highcharts. It allows you to group data by a specific unit (e.g., week, month) and display it on the chart.
* highcharts-time: This package provides additional time-related features for Highcharts, including support for different timezones and date formatting.
* momentjs: This package provides a powerful library for working with dates and times in JavaScript. It can be used to format dates and times, as well as perform calculations on them.

Here is an example of how you could use these packages to group data by week and display it on the chart:
```
// Import the necessary packages
const Highcharts = require('highcharts');
const DataGrouping = require('highcharts-data-grouping');
const Time = require('highcharts-time');

// Set up the chart with the appropriate options
Highcharts.chart('container', {
  rangeSelector: {
    allButtonsEnabled: true,
    selected: 1
  },
  plotOptions: {
    candlestick: {
      color: 'red',
      upColor: 'green'
    }
  },
  title: {
    text: 'CC Price'
  },
  series: [{
    type: 'candlestick',
    name: 'AAPL Stock Price',
    data: [
      // Your data here
    ],
    dataGrouping: {
      units: [
        ['week', [1]],
        ['month', [1, 2, 3, 4, 6]]
      ]
    }
  }]
});
```
In this example, the `dataGrouping` option is set to group data by week and month. The `units` array specifies the units to use for grouping (in this case, weeks and months), and the `allowedMultiples` array specifies the allowed multiples for each unit (e.g., [1] for weeks and [1, 2, 3, 4, 6] for months).

You can also use the `momentjs` library to format dates and times in your data before passing them to Highcharts. For example:
```
// Import the necessary packages
const Highcharts = require('highcharts');
const DataGrouping = require('highcharts-data-grouping');
const Time = require('highcharts-time');
const moment = require('moment');

// Set up the chart with the appropriate options
Highcharts.chart('container', {
  rangeSelector: {
    allButtonsEnabled: true,
    selected: 1
  },
  plotOptions: {
    candlestick: {
      color: 'red',
      upColor: 'green'
    }
  },
  title: {
    text: 'CC Price'
  },
  series: [{
    type: 'candlestick',
    name: 'AAPL Stock Price',
    data: [
      // Your data here
    ],
    dataGrouping: {
      units: [
        ['week', [1]],
        ['month', [1, 2, 3, 4, 6]]
      ]
    }
  }]
});
```
In this example, the `moment` library is used to format the dates in your data before passing them to Highcharts. The `format` method is called on each date to convert it to a string in the desired format (e.g., "YYYY-MM-DD").

Highcharts, Highstock, Highmaps, and Gantt are all JavaScript packages that can be used to create interactive charts and graphs in web applications. To use them in an Electron app, you need to include the necessary packages in your project's dependencies.

Here is a list of the packages you may need to install:

* highcharts
* highstock
* highmaps
* gantt

You can install these packages using npm by running the following command in your terminal:
```
npm install highcharts highstock highmaps gantt
```
Once you have installed the necessary packages, you can use them in your Electron app by importing and initializing them in your JavaScript code. For example:
```
const Highcharts = require('highcharts');
const Highstock = require('highstock');
const Highmaps = require('highmaps');
const Gantt = require('gantt');

// Initialize the charts
Highcharts.chart('graph', {...});
Highstock.chart('graph', {...});
Highmaps.chart('graph', {...});
Gantt.chart('graph', {...});
```
Note that you may need to adjust your Electron app's configuration to allow for the use of these packages. You can do this by setting the `nodeIntegration` option in your Electron app's `webPreferences` object to `true`. For example:
```
const electron = require('electron');
const BrowserWindow = electron.BrowserWindow;

// Create a new browser window
let win = new BrowserWindow({
  width: config.width,
  height: config.height,
  backgroundColor: "#000000",
  resizable: true,
  show: false,
  useContentSize: true,
  webPreferences: {
    backgroundThrottling: false,
    contextIsolation: false,
    nodeIntegration: true, // <-- set this to true to allow for the use of Node.js modules in your Electron app
    spellcheck: false,
    zoomFactor: desired_zoomfactor,
  }
});
```
By setting `nodeIntegration` to `true`, you are allowing your Electron app to use Node.js modules, which includes the Highcharts, Highstock, Highmaps, and Gantt packages.

