response_prompt:
To determine if an MP4 file with H264 encoding has slices enabled or not, you can check the value of the "sps_max_dec_frame_buffering" parameter in the SPS (Sequence Parameter Set) NAL unit. If this parameter is greater than 1, it indicates that slicing is enabled and multiple slices are used to encode each frame.

Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to extract the value of "sps_max_dec_frame_buffering" from an MP4 file with H264 encoding:
```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const mpegts = require('mpegts');

// Open the MP4 file for reading
const file = fs.openSync('input.mp4', 'r');

// Create a new MPEG-TS parser
const parser = new mpegts.Parser();

// Parse the MP4 file and extract the SPS NAL unit
parser.parse(file, (err, nal) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error('Error parsing MP4 file:', err);
    return;
  }

  // Check if the parsed NAL unit is an SPS
  if (nal.type === 'sps') {
    const sps = nal.data;

    // Extract the value of "sps_max_dec_frame_buffering" from the SPS
    const spsMaxDecFrameBuffering = sps[1] & 0x3F;

    console.log(`The maximum number of decoded frames in buffer is ${spsMaxDecFrameBuffering}.`);
  }
});
```
In this example, we use the `mpegts` library to parse the MP4 file and extract the SPS NAL unit. We then check if the parsed NAL unit is an SPS and extract the value of "sps_max_dec_frame_buffering" from it. If the value is greater than 1, it indicates that slicing is enabled and multiple slices are used to encode each frame.

Note that this code snippet assumes that the MP4 file has only one video track and that the SPS NAL unit is located at the beginning of the file. In a real-world scenario, you may need to handle more complex cases such as multiple video tracks or non-standard SPS parameters.

