To enable hot reload in Ember 4.2, you can use the `ember-cli` command line tool to generate a new project with the `--hot-reload` flag. Here's an example of how to do this:
```
ember new my-app --hot-reload
```
This will create a new Ember app called `my-app` with hot reload enabled.

Once you have created your app, you can enable hot reload for individual components by adding the `hotReload` option to their definition in the `components` directory. For example:
```
// components/my-component.js
import Component from '@ember/component';
import { hotReload } from 'ember-cli';

export default Component.extend({
  // component code here
}, {
  hotReload: true
});
```
This will enable hot reload for the `my-component` component.

You can also enable hot reload for all components in your app by adding the following line to your `ember-cli-build.js` file:
```
module.exports = function(environment) {
  // ...
  ENV['hotReload'] = true;
};
```
This will enable hot reload for all components in your app.

Note that hot reload is only available in Ember 4.2 and later versions. If you are using an earlier version of Ember, you may need to use a different approach to achieve the same result.

