The main difference between BrowserRouter and createBrowserRouter in react-router v6 is that BrowserRouter uses the DOM History API to update the URL and manage the history stack, while createBrowserRouter uses the browser's built-in history stack. Additionally, createBrowserRouter enables the v6.4 data APIs like loaders, actions, fetchers, etc.

You can use createBrowserRouter without adding data APIs like loaders, actions, etc. However, if you want to use Redux Toolkit Query with createBrowserRouter, you will need to add the necessary data APIs to your project.

Here is an example of how you can use createBrowserRouter in a React Router v6 project:
```
import { createBrowserRouter } from 'react-router-dom';

const router = createBrowserRouter();

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <nav>
        <ul>
          <li><Link to="/">Home</Link></li>
          <li><Link to="/about">About</Link></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
        <Route path="/about" element={<About />} />
      </Routes>
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, we are using createBrowserRouter to create a router instance and then passing it to the Routes component. The Routes component will use the router instance to navigate between routes.

You can also add data APIs like loaders, actions, etc. to your project by installing the necessary packages and configuring them in your Redux store. Here is an example of how you can add a loader to your project:
```
import { createBrowserRouter } from 'react-router-dom';
import { createLoader } from '@redux-toolkit/query';

const router = createBrowserRouter();
const loader = createLoader(router);

function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <nav>
        <ul>
          <li><Link to="/">Home</Link></li>
          <li><Link to="/about">About</Link></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
        <Route path="/about" element={<About />} />
      </Routes>
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, we are using createLoader to create a loader instance and then passing it to the Routes component. The Routes component will use the loader instance to fetch data from the server when navigating between routes.

