response_prompt:
The issue is that the initial UI does not match what was rendered on the server because the `localStorage` API is not available on the server-side. When you use `useEffect` to set the theme based on the value stored in `localStorage`, it only runs on the client-side, so the initial UI generated by the server does not have the same theme as what was rendered on the client.

To solve this issue, you can use a different approach to store and retrieve the theme preference. One way is to use cookies instead of local storage. Here's an example of how you can modify your code to use cookies:
```jsx
import { ThemeContext } from '@store/theme';
import { ReactNode, useState, useEffect } from 'react';

interface ContextProviderProps {
  children: ReactNode;
}

const ContextProvider = ({ children }: ContextProviderProps) => {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('dark');

  useEffect(() => {
    const storedTheme = Cookies.get('theme');
    if (storedTheme === 'light' || storedTheme === 'dark') {
      setTheme(storedTheme);
    } else {
      Cookies.set('theme', theme);
    }
  }, [theme]);

  const toggle = () => {
    const newTheme = theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light';
    setTheme(newTheme);
    Cookies.set('theme', newTheme);
  };

  return (
    <>
      {children}
    </>
  );
};
```
In this example, we use the `Cookies` library to store and retrieve the theme preference in a cookie instead of using local storage. This way, the initial UI generated by the server will match what was rendered on the client after the first render.

