response_prompt:
It seems like a bug in the @vitejs/plugin-vue plugin when used with Vue 3.3 and CoffeeScript. The plugin is trying to compile the CoffeeScript code as if it were JavaScript, which results in syntax errors. This behavior was not present in Vue 3.2, where the plugin would simply import and compile the CoffeeScript code without any issues.

To fix this issue, you can try adding a `lang` attribute to your `<script>` tag with the value of `coffeescript`. This tells Vite that the script is written in CoffeeScript and should be compiled accordingly. Here's an example:
```html
<script lang="coffeescript">
  export default {
    data() {
      return { count: ref(0) }
    },
    template: `
      <div>
        <button @click="count++">Count is {{ count }}</button>
      </div>
    `,
  }
</script>
```
Alternatively, you can also try using the `@vue/babel-plugin-transform-coffeescript` plugin to compile your CoffeeScript code. This plugin is specifically designed for use with Vue and should work well with Vite. Here's an example:
```js
// vite.config.js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import vue from '@vitejs/plugin-vue';
import CoffeeScript from "coffeescript";
import transformCoffeeScript from "@vue/babel-plugin-transform-coffeescript";

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    vue(),
    {
      name: 'coffee_compile',
      transform(src, id) {
        if (/\.coffee$/.test(id)) {
          const { js, sourceMap } = CoffeeScript.compile(src, { sourceMap: true });
          return { code: js, map: sourceMap };
        }
      },
    },
  ],
});
```
In this example, the `transformCoffeeScript` plugin is added to the Vite configuration and will be used to compile any CoffeeScript files found in your project. The `vue()` plugin is still used to handle Vue-specific code, but it will now also use the `transformCoffeeScript` plugin to compile any CoffeeScript code that it encounters.

I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions or issues.

