The error message you're seeing is likely due to a mismatch between the URL scheme used in your code and the Electron runtime. The `Network.loadNetworkResource` method is used to load resources from the network, but it only supports HTTP(S) URLs. When you try to load a resource with an unsupported URL scheme (in this case, "file://"), Electron will throw an error.

To fix this issue, you can try using the `electron-isomorphic-fetch` package to make your requests. This package provides a compatible implementation of the Fetch API that works in both Node.js and the browser, including Electron. You can use it like this:
```
const fetch = require('electron-isomorphic-fetch');

fetch('https://example.com')
  .then(response => response.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data));
```
This will make a GET request to the specified URL and log the JSON response to the console.

Alternatively, you can try using the `fs` module in Node.js to read the file contents directly instead of making an HTTP request. This would look something like this:
```
const fs = require('fs');

fs.readFile('file:///path/to/file', (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error(err);
  } else {
    console.log(data.toString());
  }
});
```
This will read the contents of the specified file and log them to the console as a string.

