response_prompt:
To use the latest version of `@ffmpeg/ffmpeg` in a React.js project, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the latest version of `@ffmpeg/ffmpeg` by running the following command in your terminal or command prompt:
```
npm install @ffmpeg/ffmpeg@latest
```
2. Import the `FFmpeg` class from the package and create an instance of it in your React component:
```
import { FFmpeg } from '@ffmpeg/ffmpeg';

const ffmpeg = new FFmpeg();
```
3. Use the `exec()` method to execute a command with the latest version of `@ffmpeg/ffmpeg`. For example, to add a logo to a video, you can use the following code:
```
ffmpeg.exec('-i input.mp4 -i logo.png -filter_complex "[0:v][1:v]overlay=x=10:y=10[v]" output.mp4')
  .then(() => {
    console.log('Video processed successfully');
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error(error);
  });
```
This code will add the `logo.png` image to the input video `input.mp4` at position (10, 10) and save the output video to `output.mp4`.

Note that the `exec()` method returns a promise, so you can use it with `then()` and `catch()` methods to handle the success and failure of the command execution.

Also, note that the latest version of `@ffmpeg/ffmpeg` has changed the API and functions names, so you may need to update your code accordingly. You can check the documentation for the latest version of the package to see how to use it in your React project.

