.. but I still got the same unexpected behavior.
What is the correct way to achieving independence for each instance of externalLibrary?
Again, I am a beginner with nodejs and would like to learn the best practices, so please be detailed in the explanation.

response_prompt:
The issue you are facing is due to the fact that Node.js caches modules after they have been loaded for the first time. This means that if you require the same module multiple times within your code, it will only be loaded once and any subsequent requires will return the cached version of the module.

To achieve independence for each instance of `externalLibrary`, you can use a technique called "module reloading". This involves deleting the cache for the module before requiring it again. You can do this by adding the following line of code to your constructor function:
```
delete require.cache[require.resolve("@x/y")];
```
This will delete the cached version of the `externalLibrary` module and allow Node.js to load a new instance of the module each time it is required.

Alternatively, you can also use the `require.uncache()` method to uncache the module before requiring it again. This method takes a single argument, which is the path to the module that should be uncached. For example:
```
require.uncache("@x/y");
```
This will uncache the `externalLibrary` module and allow Node.js to load a new instance of the module each time it is required.

It's important to note that using either of these techniques will cause any existing instances of the module to be garbage collected, so you should make sure that any necessary cleanup is performed before uncaching or deleting the cache.

In your case, you can add the `delete require.cache[require.resolve("@x/y")];` line of code to your constructor function in `myPanel` class, like this:
```
private constructor(panel: vscode.webviewPanel, extensionUri: vscode.Uri, someIdentifier: string) {
  // ... initialization of MyPanel ..

  delete require.cache[require.resolve("@x/y")];
  this._externalLibrary = require("@x/y");

  externalLibrary.someInitializationMethod(someIdentifier);
}
```
This will ensure that each instance of `myPanel` has its own independent copy of the `externalLibrary` module, and any changes made to one instance will not affect the other instances.

