response_prompt:
The easiest way to implement lazy loading in AngularJS is by using the `$ocLazyLoad` service provided by the `angular-oclazyload` library. This library provides a simple and easy-to-use API for lazy loading modules, controllers, services, and other dependencies.

Here's an example of how you can use `$ocLazyLoad` to load a module, controller, and service:
```
angular.module('myApp', ['ngRoute', 'oc.lazyLoad'])
  .config(['$routeProvider', function($routeProvider) {
    $routeProvider.when('/chakadChequeBookRequest', {
      title: 'chakadChequeBookRequest',
      templateUrl: root + '/partials/chakadChequeBookRequest.html',
      controller: 'chakadChequeBookRequestController',
      resolve: {
        loadModule: ['$ocLazyLoad', '$q', function($ocLazyLoad, $q) {
          let deferred = $q.defer();

          // Load the module and its dependencies
          $ocLazyLoad.load('chakadChequeBookRequestModule').then(function() {
            // Inject the controller and service into the parent module
            $ocLazyLoad.inject('chakadChequeBookRequestController');
            $ocLazyLoad.inject('chakadChequeBookRequestService');
            deferred.resolve();
          });

          return deferred.promise;
        }]
      }
    });
  }]);
```
In this example, we're using the `$routeProvider` to define a route for the `/chakadChequeBookRequest` URL. We're also defining a `loadModule` function that will be called when the route is matched. This function uses `$ocLazyLoad` to load the `chakadChequeBookRequestModule`, which contains the controller and service we want to use.

Once the module has been loaded, we inject the controller and service into the parent module using `$ocLazyLoad.inject()`. This allows us to use the controller and service in our route's template and controller.

Note that you need to include the `angular-oclazyload` library in your project for this code to work. You can install it using npm by running the following command:
```
npm install angular-oclazyload --save
```

