How to modularize imports from a barrel file that exports from multiple directories in Next.js.

I would like to use the modularizeImports option of the Next.js compiler on a library that its barrel file exports from multiple directories within the library package.
What I mean is the index.ts file for the library looks something like this:
//index.ts
export {default as SomeComponent} from './components/SomeComponent';
export {default as someFun} from './utils/someFunction';
export {default as someHook} from './hooks/someHook';

ideally I would like to have something like this
//next.config.js
 modularizeImports: {
 '@some/library': {
 transform: '@some/library/dist/(components|hooks|utils)/{{member}}',
 },
 },

but

I am not sure if this functionality is even possible
if it is I am not sure how to to write it.

I also tried passing it an array of transforms but it only accepts a single string.
Under the hood this option is implemented with https://docs.rs/handlebars/latest/handlebars/ afaik

response_prompt:
You can use the `modularizeImports` option in your `next.config.js` file to modularize imports from a barrel file that exports from multiple directories within the library package. Here's an example of how you can do this:
```
module.exports = {
  //... other config options
  modularizeImports: {
    '@some/library': {
      transform: '@some/library/dist/(components|hooks|utils)/{{member}}',
    },
  },
};
```
This will allow you to import modules from the `@some/library` package like this:
```
import SomeComponent from '@some/library/SomeComponent';
import someFun from '@some/library/utils/someFunction';
import someHook from '@some/library/hooks/someHook';
```
The `transform` option in the `modularizeImports` object specifies a Handlebars template that will be used to generate the import path for each member of the barrel file. In this case, the template is `@some/library/dist/(components|hooks|utils)/{{member}}`, which means that the import paths for each member will be generated based on the `component`, `hook`, and `util` directories within the `@some/library` package.

Note that you need to have the `modularizeImports` option enabled in your `next.config.js` file for this to work. You can also use other options like `transform` and `ignore` to customize the modularization process further.

