```
In this example, we've added a new `glsl` property to the `options` object in the `build` configuration. This property specifies that any files with the `.glsl` extension should be included in the build process.

You can also add this property to other configurations if you want GLSL files to be included in those builds as well.

Once you've added the `glsl` property, you can use it in your code like any other file type. For example:
```typescript
import { Shader } from './my-shader';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  templateUrl: './app.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./app.component.css']
})
export class AppComponent implements OnInit {
  title = 'My App';

  ngOnInit() {
    const shader = new Shader();
    // Use the shader in your code here
  }
}
```
In this example, we've imported a `Shader` class from a file called `my-shader.glsl`. This file should contain the GLSL code for our shader.

Note that you may need to install additional dependencies to use GLSL files in your Angular project. For example, you can install the `@types/glsl` package to provide type definitions for GLSL files:
```bash
npm install @types/glsl
```

