response_prompt:
It looks like you are trying to filter the options in the second dropdown based on the selected value in the first dropdown. However, the issue is that the new data is not filtered based on the value of the first dropdown.

To fix this, you can use the `did-update` hook in your second dropdown component to update the list of options when the selected value in the first dropdown changes. Here's an example of how you can do this:
```
import Component from '@glimmer/component';
import { tracked } from '@glimmer/tracking';
import { action } from '@ember/object';
import { inject as service } from '@ember/service';
import FetchService from 'hermes/services/fetch';

interface InputsTeamSelectSignature {
  Element: HTMLDivElement;
  Args: {
    selectedBU: string | null;
    selected?: string;
    onChange: (value: string, attributes?: TeamArea) => void;
    formatIsBadge?: boolean;
    placement?: Placement;
    isSaving?: boolean;
    renderOut?: boolean;
  };
}

type TeamAreas = {
  [key: string]: TeamArea;
};

export type TeamArea = {
  abbreviation: string;
  perDocDataType: unknown;
  BU: string;
};

export default class InputsTeamSelectComponent extends Component<InputsTeamSelectSignature> {
  @service("fetch") declare fetchSvc: FetchService;

  @tracked selected = this.args.selected;

  @tracked teams: TeamAreas | undefined = undefined;
  @tracked selectedBU: string | null = null;

  @computed('args.selectedBU')
  get ReFetchTeams() {
    this.selectedBU = this.args.selectedBU;
    return this.fetchteams.perform();
  }

  get icon(): string {
    let icon = "folder";
    if (this.selected && getProductId(this.selected)) {
      icon = getProductId(this.selected) as string;
    }
    return icon;
  }

  @action onChange(newValue: any, attributes?: TeamArea) {
    this.selected = newValue;
    this.args.onChange(newValue, attributes);
  }

  protected fetchteams = task(async () => {
    try {
      // Filter the teams based on the selected business unit
      console.log("parent injected value is: ", this.args.selectedBU)
      let teams = await this.fetchSvc
        .fetch("/api/v1/teams")
        .then((resp) => resp?.json());

      // Filter the teams based on the selected business unit
      const filteredTeams: TeamAreas = {};

      for (const team in teams) {
        if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(teams, team)) {
          const teamData: TeamArea | undefined = teams[team];
          if (teamData && teamData.BU === this.args.selectedBU) {
            filteredTeams[team] = teamData;
          }
        }
      }
      console.log("the filtered teams are: ", filteredTeams);
      this.teams = filteredTeams;
      console.log(this.teams);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error(err);
      throw err;
    }
  });
}
```
In the above code, we have added a `did-update` hook to the second dropdown component that will be called whenever the selected value in the first dropdown changes. In this hook, we are fetching the list of teams from the API endpoint and filtering them based on the selected business unit. We then update the `teams` property with the filtered list of teams.

Note that we have also added a `computed` property called `ReFetchTeams` to the second dropdown component. This property will be used to trigger the `fetchteams` task whenever the selected value in the first dropdown changes. We are using the `@computed` decorator to create a computed property that will automatically update the `teams` property whenever the `selectedBU` property changes.

Also, we have added an `action` called `onChange` to the second dropdown component that will be called whenever the selected value in the first dropdown changes. In this action, we are updating the `selected` property with the new value and calling the `onChange` action from the parent component.

