response_prompt:
It looks like you are trying to filter the options in the second dropdown based on the selected value in the first dropdown. However, the issue is that the new data is not filtered based on the value of the first dropdown. To achieve this dynamic filtering behavior, you can use the `onChange` action in the first dropdown to update the `selectedBU` property and then use the `ReFetchTeams` computed property to fetch the teams again with the updated selected BU.

Here's an example of how you can implement this:

1. In the first dropdown, add an `onChange` action that updates the `selectedBU` property when a new value is selected:
```handlebars
<div class="dropdown">
  <select onchange={{action "updateSelectedBU" value="target.value"}}>
    {{#each this.buOptions as |option|}}
      <option value={{option.id}}>{{option.name}}</option>
    {{/each}}
  </select>
</div>
```
2. In the second dropdown, add a `ReFetchTeams` computed property that fetches the teams again with the updated selected BU:
```handlebars
<div class="dropdown">
  <select onchange={{action "updateSelectedTeam" value="target.value"}}>
    {{#each this.teams as |team|}}
      <option value={{team.id}}>{{team.name}}</option>
    {{/each}}
  </select>
</div>
```
3. In the component file, add an `updateSelectedBU` action that updates the `selectedBU` property and triggers a re-render of the second dropdown:
```typescript
import Component from '@glimmer/component';
import { tracked } from '@glimmer/tracking';
import { action } from '@ember/object';

export default class InputsTeamSelectComponent extends Component {
  @tracked selectedBU = null;
  @tracked teams = [];

  @action updateSelectedBU(value) {
    this.selectedBU = value;
    this.fetchTeams();
  }

  fetchTeams() {
    // Fetch the teams again with the updated selected BU
  }
}
```
4. In the `fetchTeams` method, filter the teams based on the selected BU and update the `teams` property:
```typescript
import Component from '@glimmer/component';
import { tracked } from '@glimmer/tracking';
import { action } from '@ember/object';

export default class InputsTeamSelectComponent extends Component {
  @tracked selectedBU = null;
  @tracked teams = [];

  @action updateSelectedBU(value) {
    this.selectedBU = value;
    this.fetchTeams();
  }

  fetchTeams() {
    // Fetch the teams again with the updated selected BU
    const filteredTeams = this.teams.filter((team) => team.BU === this.selectedBU);
    this.teams = filteredTeams;
  }
}
```
5. In the `ReFetchTeams` computed property, filter the teams based on the selected BU and update the `teams` property:
```typescript
import Component from '@glimmer/component';
import { tracked } from '@glimmer/tracking';
import { action } from '@ember/object';

export default class InputsTeamSelectComponent extends Component {
  @tracked selectedBU = null;
  @tracked teams = [];

  @action updateSelectedBU(value) {
    this.selectedBU = value;
    this.fetchTeams();
  }

  fetchTeams() {
    // Fetch the teams again with the updated selected BU
    const filteredTeams = this.teams.filter((team) => team.BU === this.selectedBU);
    this.teams = filteredTeams;
  }
}
```
By using the `onChange` action in the first dropdown to update the `selectedBU` property and then using the `ReFetchTeams` computed property to fetch the teams again with the updated selected BU, you can achieve dynamic filtering behavior for your dropdowns.

