response_prompt:
The error message you're seeing suggests that the `title` property of your `PostModel` class is not defined, which means that Ember Data is unable to find a matching attribute on the server.

Here are some potential solutions:

1. Make sure that the `title` property is defined in your `PostModel` class. You can do this by adding the following code to your `post.js` file:
```javascript
import Model, { attr } from '@ember-data/model';

export default class PostModel extends Model {
  @attr title;
}
```
2. Make sure that you're using the correct model name in your route. In your `posts.js` file, make sure that you're importing and using the correct model:
```javascript
import Route from '@ember/routing/route';
import PostModel from '../models/post';

export default class PostsRoute extends Route {
  model() {
    return this.store.findAll(PostModel);
  }
}
```
3. Make sure that your Mirage server is configured correctly to handle the `title` property of your `PostModel`. You can do this by adding the following code to your `posts.js` file:
```javascript
import { Factory } from 'miragejs';

export default Factory.extend({
  title() {
    return faker.lorem.sentence();
  },
});
```
4. Make sure that you're using the correct model name in your template. In your `application.hbs` file, make sure that you're importing and using the correct model:
```javascript
{{! app/templates/application.hbs }}

My ember blog

{{#each @model as |post|}}
  {{post.title}}
{{/each}}
```
5. Make sure that your Mirage server is running correctly and that it's returning the correct data for your `PostModel`. You can do this by running the following command in your terminal:
```bash
ember s --watcher=none
```
This will start your Ember app without the watcher, which should allow you to see any errors that are occurring during the startup process. If you're using a different version of Ember CLI, you may need to use a different command.

You can use the following JavaScript packages to parse the fancy text from a text area:

* `emoji-regex`: A package that provides a regular expression for matching emoji characters in Unicode.
* `strip-emojis`: A package that removes emojis from a string.
* `fancy-text-parser`: A package that parses and formats fancy text, including removing special fonts and fancy texts.

You can use them as follows:
```
const emojiRegex = require('emoji-regex');
const stripEmojis = require('strip-emojis');
const fancyTextParser = require('fancy-text-parser');

// Remove emojis from the text area
const textAreaValue = document.getElementById('myTextArea').value;
const emojiFreeText = textAreaValue.replace(emojiRegex(), '');

// Remove special fonts and fancy texts
const parsedText = fancyTextParser(emojiFreeText);
```

Here are some JavaScript packages that could be helpful in solving this problem:

* `kubernetes`: This package provides a way to interact with Kubernetes clusters and manage resources such as pods, services, and deployments.
* `cilium-cli`: This package provides a command-line interface for managing Cilium clusters and services.
* `cockroachdb-client`: This package provides a client for interacting with CockroachDB databases.
* `kubernetes-utils`: This package provides utility functions for working with Kubernetes resources such as pods, services, and deployments.
* `cilium-utils`: This package provides utility functions for working with Cilium clusters and services.

You can use these packages to create a service discovery mechanism that allows pods in different clusters to communicate with each other. For example, you could create a service that maps the FQDN of a pod in one cluster to its IP address in another cluster. This would allow pods in one cluster to reach pods in another cluster using their FQDN.

Here is an example of how you could use these packages to create a service discovery mechanism:
```
const kubernetes = require('kubernetes');
const ciliumCli = require('cilium-cli');
const cockroachdbClient = require('cockroachdb-client');
const kubernetesUtils = require('kubernetes-utils');
const ciliumUtils = require('cilium-utils');

// Create a Kubernetes client for the local cluster
const k8sClient = new kubernetes.Client();

// Create a Cilium client for the remote cluster
const ciliumClient = new ciliumCli.Client({
  host: 'https://remote-cluster-url',
  token: 'your-token'
});

// Create a CockroachDB client for the local cluster
const cockroachdbClientLocal = new cockroachdbClient.Client({
  host: 'localhost',
  port: 26257,
  database: 'mydatabase'
});

// Create a CockroachDB client for the remote cluster
const cockroachdbClientRemote = new cockroachdbClient.Client({
  host: 'remote-cluster-url',
  port: 26257,
  database: 'mydatabase'
});

// Create a service discovery mechanism that maps FQDNs to IP addresses
const fqdnToIp = new Map();

k8sClient.listPods().then(pods => {
  pods.forEach(pod => {
    const fqdn = `${pod.metadata.name}.${pod.metadata.namespace}`;
    const ip = pod.status.podIP;
    fqdnToIp.set(fqdn, ip);
  });
});

ciliumClient.listServices().then(services => {
  services.forEach(service => {
    if (service.metadata.annotations['io.cilium/global-service'] === 'true') {
      const fqdn = `${service.metadata.name}.${service.metadata.namespace}`;
      const ip = service.spec.clusterIP;
      fqdnToIp.set(fqdn, ip);
    }
  });
});

// Use the service discovery mechanism to connect to CockroachDB
const cockroachdbClientLocal.connect().then(() => {
  const query = 'SELECT * FROM mytable';
  const params = [];
  cockroachdbClientLocal.query(query, params).then(result => {
    console.log(result);
  });
});

const cockroachdbClientRemote.connect().then(() => {
  const query = 'SELECT * FROM mytable';
  const params = [];
  cockroachdbClientRemote.query(query, params).then(result => {
    console.log(result);
  });
});
```
This code creates a Kubernetes client for the local cluster and a Cilium client for the remote cluster. It then uses these clients to list all pods in the local cluster and all services with the `io.cilium/global-service` annotation set to `true` in the remote cluster. It then creates a service discovery mechanism that maps FQDNs to IP addresses using the `fqdnToIp` map.

Finally, it uses the CockroachDB clients for both clusters to connect to the database and execute a query. The query is executed on both local and remote clusters, and the results are logged to the console.

Here are some JavaScript packages that can help you create a 3D tile layer for your Cesium map:

* `cesium-ion`: This package provides an easy way to access and display imagery and other data from the Cesium ion platform.
* `cesium-terrain-server`: This package allows you to generate and serve terrain tiles for use in a Cesium application.
* `cesium-3d-tile-layer`: This package provides a simple way to add 3D tile layers to your Cesium map.
* `cesium-3d-tiles`: This package provides a more advanced way to work with 3D tiles in Cesium, including support for loading and displaying large amounts of data.

You can use these packages to create a 3D tile layer for your San Francisco location by following these steps:

1. Install the necessary packages using npm or yarn.
2. Create a new Cesium map instance and add it to your HTML page.
3. Use the `cesium-ion` package to access and display imagery and other data from the Cesium ion platform.
4. Use the `cesium-terrain-server` package to generate and serve terrain tiles for use in your Cesium map.
5. Use the `cesium-3d-tile-layer` package to add a 3D tile layer to your Cesium map.
6. Use the `cesium-3d-tiles` package to load and display large amounts of data from your 3D tile layer.

Here is an example of how you might use these packages to create a 3D tile layer for your San Francisco location:
```
// Install the necessary packages using npm or yarn
npm install cesium-ion cesium-terrain-server cesium-3d-tile-layer cesium-3d-tiles

// Create a new Cesium map instance and add it to your HTML page
const viewer = new Cesium.Viewer('cesiumContainer');

// Use the cesium-ion package to access and display imagery and other data from the Cesium ion platform
const ion = new Cesium.Ion();
viewer.imageryLayers.addImageryProvider(new Cesium.IonImageryProvider({ assetId: '3d-tile-layer' }));

// Use the cesium-terrain-server package to generate and serve terrain tiles for use in your Cesium map
const terrainServer = new Cesium.TerrainServer();
viewer.terrainProvider = new Cesium.CesiumTerrainProvider({ url: 'https://your-terrain-server.com/tiles' });

// Use the cesium-3d-tile-layer package to add a 3D tile layer to your Cesium map
const tileset = new Cesium.CesiumTileset({ url: 'https://your-3d-tile-server.com/tiles' });
viewer.scene.primitives.add(tileset);

// Use the cesium-3d-tiles package to load and display large amounts of data from your 3D tile layer
const tiles = new Cesium.CesiumTiles({ url: 'https://your-3d-tile-server.com/tiles' });
viewer.scene.primitives.add(tiles);
```
Note that this is just an example, and you will need to modify it to fit your specific use case. Additionally, you may need to configure your Cesium map instance and the 3D tile server to work with your specific data and requirements.

